#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <list>

struct NPDU {
    // Add your desired fields for NPDU here, e.g., data, timestamp, etc.
};

class NPUDCollection {
private:
    std::list<NPDU> npdus;
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
    sem_t notFull, notEmpty;
    int capacity;

public:
    NPUDCollection(int capacity) : capacity(capacity), notFull(capacity), notEmpty(-capacity) {}

    void enqueue(NPDU npdu) {
        sem_wait(&notFull);
        pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
        npdus.push_back(npdu);
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
        sem_post(&notEmpty);
    }

    NPDU dequeue() {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
        while (npdus.empty()) {
            sem_wait(&notEmpty);
        }
        auto npdu = npdus.front();
        npdus.pop_front();
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
        sem_post(&notFull);
        return npdu;
    }
};
```
This implementation uses a doubly-linked list (`std::list`) to store the NPDUs, ensuring efficient insertion and deletion. A mutex (`pthread_mutex_t`) is used for locking access to the data structure to prevent race conditions during concurrent operations. Semaphores are employed as a synchronization mechanism to manage the insertion and deletion of items when the collection is full or empty, respectively.

The constructor initializes a new NPDU collection with a specified capacity, which determines how many NPDUs can be stored without overflowing the collection. The semaphores are initialized with the corresponding values for the collection's capacity and negated capacity to signal whether it is safe to insert or remove items.

The `enqueue` function uses a semaphore to check if there is room in the collection before adding an NPDU, then acquires the mutex to ensure exclusive access when modifying the list. The `dequeue` function does the opposite: it first checks whether the collection contains any items, and acquires the mutex to safely remove an item from the front of the list.

Error handling is limited in this implementation for brevity, but proper memory allocation failure handling should be implemented using standard C functions like `malloc()` or `new`. Unexpected data corruption can be minimized by designing robust NPDU structures and validating any input before adding it to the collection.

Performance optimization can be achieved by testing different synchronization strategies under varying workloads, as well as minimizing lock contention through the use of efficient synchronization mechanisms like readers-writers locks or optimistic concurrency control techniques. Additionally, CPU and memory overhead during peak traffic times may be reduced by carefully choosing data structures and algorithms for efficient operations on the collection."]

