['```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
#include <iterator>
#include <cstdlib>

void updateOrInsert(std::vector<std::string>& vec, const std::string& target, const std::string& replacement) {
    auto it = std::find(vec.begin(), vec.end(), target);
    if (it != vec.end()) {
        std::iter_swap(it, vec.begin());
        *it = replacement;
    } else {
        vec.insert(vec.begin(), replacement);
    }
}
```
In this implementation, I have chosen to use a `std::vector<std::string>`, as it provides constant-time complexity for insertion and erasure at the end (amortized O(1)). The vector is dynamically resizable and uses contiguous memory, which makes it efficient for random access and iteration.

The `std::find` function finds the first occurrence of the target string within the vector with linear time complexity (O(n)), where n is the number of elements in the vector. If the target is found, we use `std::iter_swap` to swap the target element with the beginning of the vector and then overwrite it with the replacement string.

Using `begin()` for iteration ensures that the complexity remains constant when iterating over the vector. Additionally, this solution has linear time and space complexity (O(n)) considering both searching for the target and performing the insertion/update operation.

When dealing with large vectors or frequent updates in a streaming application or high-frequency trading system, it is important to minimize memory reallocations and ensure fast access times. A `std::vector` provides these guarantees while offering efficient operations such as push_back, pop_back, insert, and erase. Other data structures like `std::list` or `std::deque` may have their use cases depending on the specific requirements of the problem, but for this task, a vector is most suitable due to its constant-time complexity for many common operations.']

